{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact head db9f9b7754bd1be79af8a20d3046c6a3af12c325 on planner/live-level-20261110, all four artifacts, for request bf1992ae under promise 8612669f and review request 5afd0c83. This head answers review ca74b4cd. Under section 7 of `docs/kernel.md`, this approval is the review of the first version of the live level. I accept the exception of ruling 4b5e0ec2 for the reasons that ca74b4cd gives. The validator admits the level, and the fold refuses a third entry. The code that runs live acts is a platform service.\n\nThe three required changes:\n\n1. The Live row of the sequenced note's section 3 table now reads \"The genesis entry and the creator's confirmation\" and \"The two creation entries only\", as this note's section 2 does. Section 9 now names both rows.\n2. One definition remains. Section 2 defines the scope's counter as section 3.3 does: it starts at the sequence of the journal's head, and each applied input adds one.\n3. The counter has its own name, `tick`. The table in section 2 separates the two counters: an item's revision, which `expected` and `revision-moved` use, and the scope's tick, which `LiveAnswer`, the live reads, the watch and live item IDs use. Section 3.3 says that each item keeps its own revision. Decision 5 and the sentences that promises 7 and 11 gain use `tick`. No `revision` remains in a place where the tick is meant.\n\nThe smaller corrections. The head takes all of them:\n\n- Section 3.5 states the two-entry sequence as a property of the design and cites section 5. It no longer claims that the identifiers note says it.\n- Section 6 withdraws the 16.5 witness on `fact` fields.\n- Section 8 drops the claim about the cutover family.\n- `touched` is gone from `LiveAnswer`.\n- Section 3.4 separates the answer `applied` from the send-result clause.\n- O1 says that the meaning of `scope-full` widens to state in memory, and that LV3 changes the comment in `contract` as a change to `contract`.\n- Section 3.1 says that the root's seed names `lane`, and that the pinned level, not the kind, selects the live turn.\n- Section 4.2 names the existing codes, which are `ProblemCode` `unsupported-definition` in `derive` and the replay result `mismatch`. It also makes LV1 name any new code as a kernel addition.\n- Source facts now name main 7177b87b.\n\nI checked each new citation at main. `ProblemCode` and `unsupported-definition` are in `packages/derive/src/validate/shape.ts:11` and `:40`, and `format-1.ts:45` uses the code to refuse `live` today. `OutboxItem` is in `packages/contract/src/read.ts:101`, `create-application` in `packages/platform/src/application.ts:105`, and `mismatch` in `packages/contract/src/report.ts:26`. Section 6 of the volatile note uses \"revision\" for the counter that this note renames.\n\nScope. Main 7177b87b is an ancestor, the head is pushed, and the planner's worktree is clean at the head. All four artifacts name the head's commit and their own paths. Against main, the head changes exactly those four paths, all Markdown. `check-notes` reports 0 warnings, and the commit-message check passes with 3 checked and 0 flagged. `git diff --check` is clean. On my own extracted copy, `check-doc-links.mjs` checks 1,228 local links and finds none broken.\n\nArchitecture: sound. A live scope is an ordinary scope with two entries, and its reference is a `SequenceRef`. The kernel part is one admission and two refusals. The live turn reuses the judges of `derive`, so a live run and a recorded run of one definition reach the same state. LV1 to LV5 follow the dependency order and keep one gate each.\n\nSecurity: no finding. A live act is a signed intent under a membership grant. A live definition cannot send, hold a capability or present a fact. No recorded guard reads live state. LV2's witness now includes the repeat that applies again after a reset.\n\nSimplification: no required change. One note for the next touch: section 3.4 says that the tick of `applied` \"is the ID of an item that the act opened\". An act that opens no item still gets a tick, so the sentence holds only when the act opens an item. Writing \"If the act opened an item, the tick is its ID\" would cover both cases.\n\nThis approval authorizes no landing order beyond the planner's own, and no build. Each LV head comes to review on its own.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:f5cc4d8a13be462afb6eb693bda431cde61c9f9f","binding":"assigned","head":"db9f9b7754bd1be79af8a20d3046c6a3af12c325","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:fe96f3d7af7d0b61e6734452f20d7fb35d68b355\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:8612669f54d4ccccf03a3ab1ab46016296a892e8\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:625c912bdccd1f9b6c044597a4b49b703b9bb1f1","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}